<div class="bg-surface border border-rule rounded-[3px] p-6">
	<h3 class="text-xl font-medium text-ink mb-1">A Rep for Delegation</h3>
	<p class="text-muted text-xs mb-4">
		Built on Albert Ellis's ABC model, the foundation of Rational Emotive
		Behavior Therapy (REBT).
	</p>
	<dl class="flex flex-col gap-4">
		<div>
			<dt class="eyebrow text-muted mb-1">A · Activating Event</dt>
			<dd class="text-ink">
				<span class="block text-muted text-sm italic mb-1">
					What exact situation just occurred?
				</span>
				You observe a mistake in progress on a task you have already delegated.
				Your instinct is to take it back and do it yourself.
			</dd>
		</div>
		<div>
			<dt class="eyebrow text-muted mb-1">B · Beliefs</dt>
			<dd class="text-ink">
				<span class="block text-muted text-sm italic mb-1">
					What rigid "must" or demand am I running?
				</span>
				Why isn't this person taking this as seriously as I do? If this doesn't
				get done right it reflects on me — and that means I'm not as competent
				as I need to be.
			</dd>
		</div>
		<div>
			<dt class="eyebrow text-muted mb-1">C · Consequences</dt>
			<dd class="text-ink">
				<span class="block text-muted text-sm italic mb-1">
					What unproductive emotion or behavior is this driving?
				</span>
				A flash of frustration, and the urge to step in and take the work
				back right now.
			</dd>
		</div>
		<div>
			<dt class="eyebrow text-muted mb-1">D · Disputing</dt>
			<dd class="text-ink">
				<span class="block text-muted text-sm italic mb-1">
					Where is the evidence that this must not happen?
				</span>
				Where is it written that delegated work has to go right the first
				time? A mistake getting corrected is not a verdict on how I lead —
				it's just a mistake getting corrected.
			</dd>
		</div>
		<div>
			<dt class="eyebrow text-muted mb-1">E · Effective Thinking</dt>
			<dd class="text-ink">
				<span class="block text-muted text-sm italic mb-1">
					What is the rational, uncoupled truth?
				</span>
				I hope they take this as seriously as I do, but if they don't, it's not
				the end of the world. My job is to develop people, not to do their work
				for them — and their mistake doesn't decide whether I'm good at mine.
			</dd>
		</div>
		<div>
			<dt class="eyebrow text-muted mb-1">F · Feeling &amp; Rep</dt>
			<dd class="text-ink">
				<span class="block text-muted text-sm italic mb-1">
					What physical, constructive move will I execute right now?
				</span>
				Before the conversation, take ten seconds to picture yourself running
				it calmly — measured, not defensive. Then have a brief, specific
				conversation with the person about what needs to be corrected and why.
				Be clear about the standard. Step back and give them 24 hours to work
				on it before you reassess. Do not check in before the 24 hours are up.
			</dd>
		</div>
	</dl>
</div>
